   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Conversion",
     "md": "A point has polar coordinates $(r,\\theta)$: $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$; $r^2=x^2+y^2$, $\\tan\\theta=\\frac yx$ (check the quadrant). The pole is the origin and $\\theta$ is measured anticlockwise from the initial line (positive x-axis)."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "propriete",
     "title": "Standard curves",
     "md": "$r=a$: circle centre $O$. $\\theta=\\alpha$: half-line. $r=a(1+\\cos\\theta)$: cardioid (heart shape; $r=0$ at $\\theta=\\pi$). $r=a\\cos\\theta$: circle through $O$ with diameter $a$ (Cartesian: $x^2+y^2=ax$). $r=a\\cos2\\theta$: four-petalled rose. To sketch, tabulate $r$ for several $\\theta$ and look for symmetry and where $r=0$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "formule",
     "title": "Area",
     "md": "The area of the region enclosed by the curve $r=f(\\theta)$ and the half-lines $\\theta=\\alpha$, $\\theta=\\beta$ is $A=\\frac{1}{2}\\int_\\alpha^\\beta r^2\\,d\\theta$. For $\\cos^2$ terms use $\\cos^2\\theta=\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\cos2\\theta)$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "Forgetting the $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the area formula. Using limits that go round the curve more than once. Treating $r<0$ as impossible (it is a point in the opposite direction)."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Convert the polar point $\\left(4,\\frac\\pi3\\right)$ to Cartesian coordinates and write $r=2\\cos\\theta$ in Cartesian form.",
     "steps": [
      "$x=4\\cos\\frac\\pi3=2$, $y=4\\sin\\frac\\pi3=2\\sqrt3\\approx3.46$.",
      "$r=2\\cos\\theta$: multiply by $r$: $r^2=2r\\cos\\theta$, so $x^2+y^2=2x$ (a circle)."
     ],
     "answer": "$(2,2\\sqrt3)$; $x^2+y^2=2x$"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2",
     "statement": "Find the area enclosed by the cardioid $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta)$.",
     "steps": [
      "$A=\\frac{1}{2}\\int_0^{2\\pi}4(1+\\cos\\theta)^2d\\theta=2\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(1+2\\cos\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta\\right)d\\theta$.",
      "$\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta=0$ and $\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2\\theta\\,d\\theta=\\pi$, so $A=2(2\\pi+0+\\pi)=6\\pi$."
     ],
     "answer": "$6\\pi\\approx18.85$",
